   "l": "Bearing the bodily soul, embracing the One —\ncan you be without parting?",
   "v": true,
   "i": "Keeping the bodily soul, holding to the One — can you keep the two from coming apart?",
   "n": "Six questions in a row begin here, all built the same way: 能 'are you able' … 乎, a particle at the end that does all the asking. Nothing else marks the question — no inversion, no change of order — which is why 乎 is worth watching for. 載 is a genuine crux: as a verb it is 'to carry, to bear', and Wang Bi glosses it 處 'to dwell in', but it is also a particle, and some readers attach it to the last line of chapter 9 instead, so that this chapter opens at 營魄. The chapter divisions are traditional, so there is nothing to appeal to. 營魄 is the bodily soul, the one that stays with the body; Chinese counted a second, 魂 hún, that goes up. 一 'the One' is the undivided — the Way before anything has been told apart from anything.",
   "p": true
