   "l": "Gives life and yet does not possess,\nacts and yet does not presume on it,\nfosters and yet does not master —\nthis is called dark potency.",
   "v": true,
   "i": "It gives life and does not own, acts and does not presume on what it has done, brings things on without making itself their master: this is what is called the dark potency.",
   "n": "You have met the first two of these lines already, in chapter 2. 長 is the trap here: zhǎng, 'to be senior over, to bring on to growth' — not the cháng 'long' of chapters 2, 7 and 9. One character, two readings, two meanings, nothing on the page to part them. 德 is the book's other title-word: 道德經 is the classic of 道 and 德, and our English for it is fixed at potency — the power a thing has simply by being what it is, not 'virtue' in the moral sense. 是謂 'this is called' is a formula: the demonstrative 是 as subject, 謂 as verb, and no copula anywhere in it."
